The following code snippet contains a bug:

```php
    public function redirect()
    {
        return $this->status() >= 300 && $this->status() < 400;
    }
```

The code fails the following tests:

Test `Illuminate\Tests\Http\HttpClientTest::testUnauthorizedRequest`:
            
```php
    public function testUnauthorizedRequest()
    {
        $this->factory->fake([
            'laravel.com' => $this->factory::response('', 401),
        ]);

        $response = $this->factory->post('http://laravel.com');

        $this->assertTrue($response->unauthorized());
    }
```

The error message is:

```
Call to undefined method GuzzleHttp\Psr7\Response::unauthorized()
```
Test `Illuminate\Tests\Http\HttpClientTest::testForbiddenRequest`:
            
```php
    public function testForbiddenRequest()
    {
        $this->factory->fake([
            'laravel.com' => $this->factory::response('', 403),
        ]);

        $response = $this->factory->post('http://laravel.com');

        $this->assertTrue($response->forbidden());
    }
```

The error message is:

```
Call to undefined method GuzzleHttp\Psr7\Response::forbidden()
```

Please provide a fixed version of the buggy function, and only that function without additional imports. Make sure the code is between ``` and ```.
To address the issues in the provided code, we need to implement methods `unauthorized()` and `forbidden()` in the class. These methods should check if the status code corresponds to unauthorized (401) and forbidden (403) responses, respectively. Here's how you can fix the `redirect()` method and add the required methods:

```php
public function redirect()
{
    return $this->status() >= 300 && $this->status() < 400;
}

public function unauthorized()
{
    return $this->status() === 401;
}

public function forbidden()
{
    return $this->status() === 403;
}
```

This code will now correctly handle the checks for redirection, unauthorized, and forbidden responses.
